 "inputs_sha256": "76faee5cba3cdeffa4e98bebda914b69e5dab4d83ae4e713523759256d355d3e",
 "caveats": [
  "The light is shared in the Kepler band with Gaia G as the proxy: the package's own shares use TIC TESS magnitudes (Tmag), which understate a G-type neighbour's share beside a red target in the Kepler band. The package's bound-companion and background-star dilution stay in the TESS band, a known approximation with a direction each way: a bound companion of a cool dwarf is cooler still, so the TESS band overstates its light and the dilution it causes, while background field stars are mostly bluer than the target, so the TESS band understates theirs. The limb darkening comes from the package's Kepler table.",
  "No high-resolution imaging exists, so the package lets an unresolved bound companion sit anywhere within 2.2 arcsec of the target; a planet transiting such a companion (STP) counts towards the full FPP and is the open false-positive channel. SkySift's model has no such scenario, so the FPP without STP, (FPP - P_STP) / (1 - P_STP), is quoted beside the full FPP.",
  "Giacalone et al. 2021's classes are labels only: the paper found no validation region at 30-minute cadence (its section 4.2), which Kepler long cadence is, so no candidate is called validated by this tool.",
  "The priors are the package's own, not SkySift's, and the field-star population is the Gaia DR3 field-star population TRICERATOPS 1.1.0 uses by default, not the 2021 paper's TRILEGAL one.",
  "The result varies from run to run, so the median and the 16th to 84th percentiles over the runs that are not degenerate are quoted, and every run is kept.",
  "The dossier's Gaia neighbours are matched to the TIC stars by their separation from the target only, since the dossier stores no position for them.",
  "No star is removed on the strength of SkySift's own centroid test, which would make the cross-check circular.",
  "Gaia DR3 2077554747134280320: Teff, rad, mass set from Mamajek dwarf table at BP-RP 0.850 (Pecaut, M. J. and Mamajek, E. E. 2013, ApJS 208, 9), since the TIC row lacks them.",
  "Gaia DR3 2077554747134279552: Teff, rad, mass set from Mamajek dwarf table at BP-RP 0.378 (Pecaut, M. J. and Mamajek, E. E. 2013, ApJS 208, 9), since the TIC row lacks them.",
  "Gaia DR3 2077554811556745344: Teff, rad, mass set from Mamajek dwarf table at BP-RP 0.907 (Pecaut, M. J. and Mamajek, E. E. 2013, ApJS 208, 9), since the TIC row lacks them.",
  "CROWDSAP 0.8849 of the target's KBonus file is the target's fraction of the aperture light, so the light the neighbours leak into its KBonus PSF light curve is at most (1 - CROWDSAP) / CROWDSAP = 0.1301 of the target's own (the target's flux ratio is CROWDSAP), shared among them by the package's Gaussian-box fractions in the Kepler band (Gaia G as the proxy). The PSF fit removes that light to first order, so each share, and the nearby false-positive probability with it, is an upper bound.",
  "SkySift's leak test measures the dip in the neighbour's own aperture (40 +- 33 ppm in KIC 6699878), the opposite direction to the leak into this star's light curve, so it is a consistency check and not a share.",
